set

set is a built-in shell command used to change or configure internal shell options and positional parameters.

It allows you to modify the behavior of your current shell environment or script execution on the fly. When you turn a shell option "on" or "off", you change how Bash handles errors, variables, wildcards, and history.


How to Toggle Options: The + and - Syntax

The syntax for the set command can be highly confusing to beginners because it uses a minus sign to turn options ON, and a plus sign to turn options OFF:
• set -o option_name (or set -letter): Turns an option ON (Enables it).
• set +o option_name (or set +letter): Turns an option OFF (Disables it).


The Most Common Shell Options Every Admin Uses

These are the most critical settings used in production-grade Bash scripts to make them safer and easier to debug:

1. Exit Immediately on Error (-e or errexit)

By default, if a line in a Bash script fails, Bash will stubbornly ignore it and keep executing the next line.
• Enable: set -e
• Result: The script stops and exits immediately if any command returns a non-zero (error) status. This prevents a failed cd command from causing a subsequent rm -rf * to wipe out the wrong directory.

2. Treat Unset Variables as an Error (-u or nounset)

By default, if you reference a variable that hasn't been defined, Bash treats it as completely blank empty space.
• Enable: set -u
• Result: The script will crash with an "unbound variable" error if you try to use a variable you didn't define. This catches typing mistakes (e.g., typing $FILNAME instead of $FILENAME).

3. Catch Errors in Pipelines (-o pipefail)

Normally, if you run a pipeline like invalid_command | echo "Hello", the total command succeeds because the last command (echo) succeeded.
• Enable: set -o pipefail
• Result: The pipeline returns a failure status if any command inside the chain fails, not just the last one.

4. Print Commands as They Run (-x or xtrace)

This is the ultimate debugging tool for Linux scripts.
• Enable: set -x
• Result: Prints every single command to the screen exactly as it expands and runs, allowing you to trace exactly what your script is doing step-by-step.

